WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Inside every desktop computer, smartphone, gaming console, laptop, or practically any

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:13.000
other device you use on a daily basis is a CPU or central processing unit and in

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
this video we're going to see how they work. A typical processor for a powerful

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000
laptop like this one is built from billions of nanoscopic transistors

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:28.000
connected together using dozens of layers of wires and is essentially the

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.000
brain of the device. But before we explore the microprocessor and all its

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:39.000
complexity, let's travel to the early days of personal computers and video game

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:46.000
consoles and compare the Apple IIe from 1983 to the modern-day MacBook Pro.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Inside the Apple IIe we find a chip called a 6502 which is considered one of the

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
great-grandparents of all modern processors. This chip is built from

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:07.000
4,528 transistors and can perform around 430,000 calculations a second. While it

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.000
could only run primitive applications in video games with simple graphics, this chip

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
was the backbone of a generation of early computers and video game consoles such as

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:36.000
the NES, the Commodore 64, and the Atari. Compare that to the MacBook Pro's M1 processor which is built from 16 billion transistors capable of performing around 3 trillion calculations a second, thus enabling it to generate

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:45.000
expansive 3D worlds with immersive graphics. Despite these two chips being released around 45 years apart,

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:53.000
the underlying principles of how they work are rather similar. In a way, you can think of these devices as sharing a common section of technological DNA.

00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:03.000
In fact, if we were to open up a desktop computer and grab the CPU or the GPU inside the graphics card,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:12.000
or tear down a Nintendo Switch or smartphone and find the system on a chip or SoC, or even if you could make your way into an AI

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:22.000
data center and grab a state of the art AI chip, you'd find that all of these processors operate using the same underlying principles.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:31.000
In other words, both the Oregon Trail of 50 years ago and advanced AI algorithms run on processors with similar

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:41.000
technological DNA. But of course, one of these chips is 10 billion times more computationally powerful than the other.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.000
So in this video, we're going to take apart this microprocessor and find out exactly what the shared

00:02:47.000 --> 00:03:01.000
technological DNA is and how it enables CPUs to work. And just to be clear, the technological DNA is not transistors and it's not logic gates.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:13.000
But rather, it's an architectural design and basic operational principle that's fundamental to microprocessors and differentiates these chips from other integrated circuits.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:24.000
So stick around and let's dive right in.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.000
This video is sponsored by Brilliant.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:32.000
Let's begin with a quick 3D animated teardown of this MacBook Pro.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:43.000
When we open it up, we find a range of different components, such as the touchpad, battery cells, speakers, a cooling fan, and the motherboard and the

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:54.000
center. Mounted to the motherboard are the solid state drive or SSD storage chips, where all your files are saved, and a range of other chips.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:01.000
Underneath the heat pipe, we find the DRAM, which is the short-term working memory and central processing unit.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Let's desolder the DRAM and CPU and open it up.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:19.000
Inside, we find three parts. On the top is a protective cover that conducts and dissipates heat. On the bottom is an interposer with thousands of connection points on either side and wires running inside of it.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:30.000
And soldered onto the interposer is the integrated circuit, or IC, which is also called a die, and is the functional part of the CPU.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:41.000
On the die, we can see the complex design of billions of transistors and wires organized into different sections, such as the four high-performance computational cores,

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:48.000
four energy-efficient cores, graphics processing cores, cache memory, and many other sections.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Let's zoom in on one of the performance cores, where we find that it's separated into different functional blocks, which we'll add labels to and then reorganize into an architectural diagram.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:16.000
This diagram illustrates how data and instructions move around a single processing core in the CPU, and, although it's rather complicated, you'll understand how it works by the end of this video.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:26.000
But for now, let's zoom in even further to get a nanoscopic view of a massive multilayer labyrinth of wires with the transistors at the very bottom.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Here we see a group of six transistors that are wired together to build an AND logic gate.

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:42.000
And in this view, we can see around 650 transistors out of the total 16 billion that make up the overall chip.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:55.000
Understanding how billions of transistors work together to build a CPU capable of playing video games, watching movies, or browsing the internet will take a bit of work.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.000
So, let's start with an analogy.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:02.000
You may have heard that CPUs are like super powerful calculators.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:08.000
This analogy is only around 20% complete, as it's missing some critical parts.

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:12.000
So, let's add them in to make a more accurate analogy.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:18.000
First, we'll add a table for the calculator to sit on, along with a pencil and a sheet of paper.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Next, we'll add rows and rows of bookshelves containing thousands of books, along with a cart that can carry a small stack of books between the shelves and the table.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:38.000
And finally, we'll add an automated robot, which we'll call a control unit or controller.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:45.000
The controller can grab books from the bookshelves, move them to the cart and onto the table, and it can put them back.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:54.000
The controller can also read the contents of each book, write on the paper and in the books, and use the calculator.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:07:03.000
You can think of the controller as a super-fast human, but we're bad at animating humans, so it's a robot instead.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.000
And with that, we have all the parts for our analogy.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Now let's see how each part of our CPU analogy works.

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:18.000
To start, the bookshelves of the storage devices in your computer, such as the SSD chips.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:25.000
The cart represents the DRAM, and the table and its contents represent the CPU.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:36.000
On the CPU table, there's a small space for a single open book, which is similar to the very limited capacity of the cache memory inside the CPU itself.

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:46.000
Next, the single sheet of paper represents the registers which are used for storing values or numbers that are actively being used.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:55.000
Specifically, on it are four general-purpose registers, and a few more special locations, which we'll discuss in a little bit.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:01.000
Additionally, the pencil is there to write and erase things on the paper and in the books.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:07.000
Finally, the calculator represents the arithmetic logic unit, or ALU.

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:14.000
This ALU calculator works using binary, so there are only the digits 0 and 1,

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:20.000
and it can do simple functions like add, subtract, or multiply two numbers.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:27.000
The ALU calculator has many more functions that you may be unfamiliar with, but are still rather simple.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:34.000
For example, it can increase or decrease a number by 1, or the ALU can perform bit shifts,

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:39.000
which is essentially taking a number and adding a 0 to the end of it.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:46.000
In decimal, bit shifting is like multiplying a number by 10, but in binary, it's equivalent to multiplying a number by 2.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:57.000
The ALU can also perform logic functions on two numbers, such as the logical AND, OR, or exclusive OR operations.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:09.000
For example, here is the logical AND operation for two binary inputs, and you can see that the output is a logical AND for each place value of the two inputs.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:15.000
However, more importantly, the ALU calculator can perform comparisons.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:22.000
For example, you can input two numbers and hit the comparison button to test whether the numbers are equal to one another.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:32.000
And if they are, then an equals flag goes up, while the other comparison flags, like less than or greater than, stay down.

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:40.000
Finally, the ALU calculator's display that outputs the result has a special name called the accumulator.

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:46.000
So, now that we've explained the various parts of this analogy, how does it all work together?

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:54.000
Well, the first step is to load a program that we want to run, which is like moving a set of books from the bookshelves to the cart,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:59.000
and then moving a single book to the table, and opening it up.

00:09:59.000 --> 00:10:08.000
It's important to note that the DRAM cart and cache memory on the table are both temporary and limited capacity locations.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:15.000
Whereas the SSD bookshelves can hold a lot more and are semi-permanent, long-term storage.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:21.000
Additionally, when the computer is turned off, there are no books in the DRAM or the CPU.

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:28.000
But when the computer turns on, the cart and table are very actively shuffling books around.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.000
Let's take a look at the contents of one of the books.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:37.000
Essentially, there are two types of pages: instructions and data.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:44.000
You can think of the instructions as the directions in a cookbook, with each step numbered sequentially across the pages.

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:54.000
And the pages of data contain a list of addresses with values stored at each address, and are like the ingredients that go into the recipe itself.

00:10:54.000 --> 00:11:00.000
Similar to cooking, you need both the recipe and ingredients to make it work.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:07.000
And just a few ingredients can be combined in dozens of different ways, using different recipes.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:16.000
But to not use an analogy inside another analogy, let's drop the cooking one and focus on the books, table, and calculator.

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:26.000
Let's start at the beginning of this program and flip to page one, instruction one, which is called a load, and is the most common type of instruction.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:32.000
This load has us open the pages of data and find a specific address.

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:41.000
We then copy and write down the value stored at that address into one of the general purpose registers on the sheet of paper.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:50.000
With instruction one completed, we move to instruction two, which is to increment the value in register zero by one.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:56.000
So we plug the value into the ALU calculator and hit plus one.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:10.000
The third instruction, called a store instruction, is used to save or store the output of the calculator found in the accumulator display into the pages of data in the same address it was in before.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:16.000
These simple, yet very common instructions are equivalent to this line of code.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:23.000
Next, we move on to instruction four and complete it, and then instruction five, and so on.

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:28.000
Moving through the list of instructions, which goes on and on and on.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:41.000
In order to keep track of which instruction is the next one to be completed, we use one of the special locations on the sheet of paper that we mentioned earlier, called the program counter or PC.

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:46.000
Also called an instruction address register or instruction pointer.

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:56.000
Since the PC currently has a value of five, we find instruction five, complete it, and increase the program counter by one.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:01.000
Therefore, the next instruction to be completed will be instruction six.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:09.000
However, what if after completing instruction six, we want to jump directly to instruction 42?

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:20.000
Well, to do this, we use a jump instruction at seven, which directly sets the value in the program counter to a new number, and in this case, it's 42.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:32.000
As a result, the sequence of instructions will be five, six, seven, which is the jump instruction, then 42, 43, 44, and so on.

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:42.000
Another set of instructions is called a conditional branch, which is used for implementing if statements, loops, and other conditional code.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:48.000
Let's use an example of a for loop with a few simple lines of code inside of it.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:54.000
Quite simply, this loop is used to repeat the code inside of it four times.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.000
So, let's take a look at the instructions.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:00.000
Let's take a look at the instructions for the code inside of it.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:08.000
And we color coded each of the elements to keep track of which specific lines of code result in the corresponding instructions.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:13.000
We'll discuss how compilers turn code into instruction later in this video.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:17.000
But for now, let's focus on the for loop and its instructions.

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:24.000
Specifically, here's where i gets set to zero and stored in an address in the pages of data.

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:29.000
Here's where i is loaded from that address and incremented by one.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:32.000
And here's the contents of the loop.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:40.000
At the top, you can see the three instructions: load, compare, and branch greater than or equal to.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:46.000
The load first grabs the value for i and places it into register zero.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:58.000
Compare feeds i stored in register zero and a value of four into the ALU and compares them, resulting in the applicable comparison flags being triggered.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:07.000
Next, branch greater than or equal to checks whether either the greater than or the equals flag is on.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:15.000
It sets the program counter to 23, which corresponds to completing and leaving the loop.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:26.000
However, when i is less than 4, those flags aren't triggered and the loop continues until it hits the jump instruction at address 22,

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:32.000
where the jump sets the program counter to 6, which corresponds to the top of the for loop.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:37.000
As a result, the loop will repeat a total of four times.

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:42.000
Note that this code on the left is in C++,

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:49.000
whereas the actual instructions completed by your CPU are in a binary language called machine code.

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:54.000
And the semi-readable version of the instructions is called assembly.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:58.000
But we modified this assembly a little bit to make it more readable.

00:15:58.000 --> 00:16:07.000
One interesting note is that you may think that with everything a computer can do, there must be tens of thousands of different instructions.

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:17.000
Well, actually, the 6502 processor in the Apple IIe from 1983 could only complete 56 different instructions,

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:23.000
whereas the modern M1 chip in the MacBook Pro can complete 354 instructions.

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:28.000
Here's the list of all the instructions each chip can execute.

00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:33.000
And if you take a good look, most of these instructions are rather simple.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.000
Let's just think about that for a second.

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:46.000
Every single thing you do on your computer can be constructed using only various sequences of 354 different instructions.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:55.000
However, many programs have millions upon millions of lines of instructions, and hopefully there aren't any bugs in them.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:03.000
So, now that we've discussed the range of possible instructions, let's further explore how CPUs work.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:08.000
In order to complete an instruction, there are always three key steps:

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:11.000
Fetch, Decode, and Execute.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:22.000
The first step is Fetch, and is where the controller uses the value in the program counter to search through the pages of instructions in the book for the corresponding instruction address.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:32.000
The controller then copies the instruction found at that address into a special location called the Current Instruction Register, or CIR.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:37.000
At the same time, the controller increases the program counter by one.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:45.000
The second step is Decode, and in this step the current instruction is fed into a circuit called the instruction decoder.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:18:05.000
In our analogy from earlier, this decoder is a key part of the controller, and in essence, it's the circuitry that reads in an instruction, and both interprets what the machine code of an instruction actually does, and simultaneously produces the control signals to properly execute that instruction.

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:21.000
Specifically, this instruction decoder circuit uses the binary values of the instruction, and an incredibly complex arrangement of logic gates to produce the corresponding control signals, which are then sent to the different elements in the CPU.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:30.000
Instruction decoders are one of the more complicated parts of the CPU, but here's an example along with a simplified explanation.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:39.000
Let's say we have this ADD instruction in the Current Instruction Register, or CIR, and it's fed into the instruction decoder.

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:45.000
The first part of the binary instruction specifies we want to use the ALU.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:19:02.000
With the ALU selected, the next three bits specify that we want to use the ADD function, and then the last four bits of the instruction indicates we want the values in register 0 and register 1 to be routed and sent to the ALU.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:10.000
Instruction decoding is considerably more complicated than that, but let's move on to the third step, which is Execute.

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:29.000
During Execute, using our example instruction, the control signals from the instruction decoder and an intricate set of electrical timing signals are used to first send the value in register 0 and then the value in register 1 to the ALU.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:44.000
The Timing signals are used to accommodate the time it takes electricity to travel from the registers to the ALU and for transistors and logic gates to change their state, thereby ensuring the correct result at the output.

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:57.000
After the values are input, the ALU adds the two numbers together, and a subsequent timing signal saves the result into the accumulator, thus completing the Execute step.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:11.000
These three steps: Fetch, Decode, and Execute are used to complete a single line of instructions, and once it's completed, these steps repeat by using the new value in the program counter.

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:18.000
In essence, Fetch, Decode, and Execute form a cycle, so let's run through it again.

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:31.000
During Fetch, the controller uses the program counter's value to fetch the corresponding instruction and places it in the CIR and the program counter increases by 1.

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:43.000
Next, during Decode, the instructions binary is fed into the instruction decoder, where a complex set of logic gates generate the correct electrical control signals for that instruction.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:21:04.000
Finally, during Execute, the instruction is completed using the control signals and timing signals, and in this case, the value in the accumulator is stored back into a memory address which, using the analogy, is like writing the value from the calculator display into a data location in the book.

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:13.000
Then, the Fetch, Decode, Execute cycle repeats again using the next program counter's value, and so on.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:24.000
The Fetch, Decode, Execute cycle is used in every processor, no matter whether it's the 6502 in the Apple IIe or the M1 in the MacBook Pro.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:36.000
But, of course, there are many differences such as the size of the cache, the registers, or functions on the ALU calculator, and much more.

00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:46.000
We'll cover these differences in a few minutes, but for now, one important detail is that the Fetch, Decode, Execute cycle uses your computer's clock to regulate its pace.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:58.000
The 6502 chip had a 1 MHz clock, which ticked away at a million times a second, and thus each step in the Fetch, Decode, Execute cycle took a microsecond.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:09.000
Additionally, the 6502 was an 8-bit processor, meaning the size of the registers in the ALU's input and output were 8 bits wide.

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:24.000
On the other hand, the M1 chip is a 64-bit processor, so it can handle much larger numbers, and it uses a 3.2 GHz clock, and therefore each step takes a third of a nanosecond.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:43.000
Additionally, the M1 chip, along with all modern chips, uses a technique called pipelining, where multiple instructions are queued up, resulting in Fetch, Decode, and Execute for different program counter values and different instructions being completed at the same time.

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:53.000
There are many other optimizations in modern processors that we will soon discuss, but it's important to understand that from the second you turn on your laptop,

00:22:53.000 --> 00:23:06.000
smartphone, gaming console, GPU, or AI server, to the second you shut it off, the processor is continuously cycling through Fetch, Decode, Execute, over and over,

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:13.000
using programs filled with instructions and data, along with the CPU's clock to regulate its pace.

00:23:13.000 --> 00:23:25.000
In essence, this Fetch, Decode, Execute cycle is the common section of technological DNA that has powered every single processor built over the past 50 years.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:38.000
This cycle of steps is incredibly powerful, capable of performing trillions to quadrillions of mathematical operations every second in a single chip.

00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:44.000
But you may be wondering, are there alternatives to the Fetch, Decode, Execute cycle?

00:23:44.000 --> 00:24:00.000
Well, there's a world of different kinds of microchips. But specifically, alternatives include application-specific integrated circuits, or ASICs, such as these microchips found in this Bitcoin mining computer,

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:09.000
or field-programmable gate arrays, or FPGAs, which are the main chips in a number of automotive computers and cameras.

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:21.000
Both ASICs and FPGAs don't use the Fetch and Decode steps, but rather they perform repetitive operations by flowing data through a set pattern of logic gates and execution units,

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:25.000
making them highly optimized, but very inflexible.

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:34.000
And then even further from these chips are quantum computers, which are based on qubits and quantum circuits, which we'll discuss in future videos.

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:44.000
But now that we've covered the Fetch, Decode, Execute cycle, it's important to discuss two more steps, which are memory and write-back.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:54.000
Memory is analogous to moving books from the SSD bookshelves onto the DRAM cart, and then onto the table or cache memory.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:25:06.000
Write-back is like writing data into the books, and when space for a new book is needed on the table, the old book is placed back on the DRAM cart and eventually returned to the bookshelves.

00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:14.000
These two steps use another special location, called the memory address register, and are critical to a functioning computer.

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:26.000
But they typically take a lot longer to complete than the Fetch, Decode, Execute steps, and therefore in some architectures and textbooks they're included in the cycle, and sometimes they aren't.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:33.000
We're working on a separate video on how data moves around these memory locations, so stay tuned.

00:25:33.000 --> 00:26:00.000
Now that we've uncovered the technological DNA inside all processors, it's important to note that, similar to the DNA found in the nucleus of the cell, and there being multiple layers of biological organization and structure for all living things, there are many layers of complexity or abstraction between the Fetch, Decode, Execute cycle, and a computer running a video game or browsing the internet.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:12.000
