00:31:05.000 --> 00:31:15.000
In the final section of this video, we'll discuss this diagram we showed earlier, and the architecture of modern processors such as the M1.

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:25.000
In contrast, the analogy we've laid out is rather simple, and you're probably thinking that there must be more components than an actual CPU.

00:31:25.000 --> 00:31:32.000
In fact, this analogy is actually pretty close to what's happening inside an Apple IIe computer.

00:31:32.000 --> 00:31:41.000
Specifically, the floppy drives are the bookshelves, and then when we open up this computer, we see the DRAM chips, which are the cart.

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:54.000
And then, going inside the 6502 processor, we find an integrated circuit, or die, which has the corresponding sections that will organize into an internal architectural diagram.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:32:06.000
In this diagram, you can see the instruction decoder, ALU, the program counter, current instruction register, the other registers, and a few other sections.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:17.000
Specifically, here's where the program counter is used to fetch an instruction, and here's where the instructions and data from the DRAM chips are bussed in and out.

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:24.000
Finally, here's where the instructions are decoded, and the control signals are generated.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:36.000
One note is that there's no cache in the 6502, because the DRAM chips in the 80s were just as fast as the instructions, so the table in the analogy is even smaller.

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:45.000
As we said at the beginning of this video, the 6502 chip is made from 4528 transistors.

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:51.000
So, let's see what an M1 chip with 16 billion transistors would look like.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:56.000
To start, we have to significantly increase the size of this table.

00:32:56.000 --> 00:33:05.000
Next, we have to section off areas for each of the performance and energy efficient cores, the GPU, and other areas.

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:10.000
When we focus on one of the performance cores, we see the complex diagram from earlier.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:16.000
So let's discuss how this diagram compares to our analogy.

00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:22.000
Specifically, there's a separate set of 64 kilobyte data and instruction caches.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:28.000
As mentioned earlier, there's a pipeline to queue eight instructions per clock cycle

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:34.000
and additional sections like a branch predictor to reduce issues with conditional branching

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:39.000
and help the pipeline run smoothly. Here you can see the pipelined instruction decoder

00:33:39.000 --> 00:33:45.000
and 32 general purpose registers. One key difference is that the calculator is broken

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:51.000
up into eight separate smaller calculators, each handling a few functions. Additionally,

00:33:51.000 --> 00:33:58.000
there's a special section for load and store instructions. This is the layout of just a single core.

00:33:58.000 --> 00:34:03.000
And there are entirely different architectures in the graphics processing unit,

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:06.000
as well as inside the neural processing unit.

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:11.000
One important note is that the inclusion of these three types of processors,

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:15.000
along with hardware accelerators, such as the media engine,

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:22.000
makes this M1 chip closer to a system on a chip, or SoC, than a traditional CPU.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:28.000
Similarly, all the processors in these devices, including the CPU in your desktop computer,

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:30.000
can be considered SoCs.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:35.000
And therefore, the difference is more a marketing term than a technical one.

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:40.000
On a separate note, it's important to mention that the M1, along with all modern processors,

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:43.000
are proprietary designs and knowledge.

00:34:43.000 --> 00:34:51.000
And therefore, the diagrams we've shown are close approximations that we built using input from industry experts.

00:34:51.000 --> 00:34:58.000
Let's finally discuss our analogy in the terms of GPU chips found in graphics cards.

00:34:58.000 --> 00:35:04.000
We have a separate video covering how graphics cards work, but with respect to this analogy,

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:11.000
a GPU CUDA core is actually very similar in complexity to the architecture of the 6502.

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:17.000
Therefore, with 10,000 to 20,000 CUDA cores in a single GPU chip,

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:21.000
it's like having a massive array of 6502 cores.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:30.000
The difference is that GPUs typically use 32-bit ALU calculators and perform single-instruction multiple-thread,

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:38.000
or SIMT calculations, where a single instruction is fetched, decoded, and then distributed to a batch of cores.

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:43.000
And then those cores execute that instruction using different addresses and data.

00:35:43.000 --> 00:35:52.000
However, there are many more nuances to SIMT and GPU architecture, so let's wrap up this video on how CPUs work.

